		<wp:meta_key><![CDATA[character_description]]></wp:meta_key>
		<wp:meta_value><![CDATA[Ratnavali represents the psychological tempest that occurs when individual emotional needs push against traditional societal shorelines (Cheliyali Katta). Viswanadha uses her internal conflict, vulnerabilities, and choices to examine the moral weight placed upon women in a shifting socio-cultural landscape.]]></wp:meta_value>
		</wp:postmeta>
							<wp:postmeta>
		<wp:meta_key><![CDATA[character_description]]></wp:meta_key>
		<wp:meta_value><![CDATA[Ranga Rao acts as a personification of the "shoreline" itself—representing stability, social expectations, and classical restraint. His interactions with Ratnavali highlight the friction between unyielding traditional duty and modern personal freedom.]]></wp:meta_value>
